  "text": "Allophone: A phonological variant of a phoneme that occurs in a specific environment and is not contrastive.\n\nAllophyll: A term not commonly used in linguistics; may refer to a sound that is not a phoneme in a particular language.\n\nAllophony: The process by which a phoneme is realized as different allophones in different contexts.\n\nAmbiguous sentence: A sentence that can be interpreted in more than one way due to structural or semantic ambiguity.\n\nAblaut: A type of internal regular consonant or vowel alternation in the root of a word, often seen in inflectional morphology.\n\nAccent: The stress or emphasis placed on a syllable or word within a phrase or utterance.\n\nAffix: A morpheme that is added to another morpheme to form a word, such as prefixes, suffixes, or infixes.\n\nAgglutinative language: A language in which words are formed by adding morphemes to a root, with each morpheme typically corresponding to a single meaning.\n\nAllophone: A phonological variant of a phoneme that occurs in a specific environment and is not contrastive.\n\nAblative case: A grammatical case indicating the source or origin of something, commonly found in languages with case systems.\n\nAffixation: The process of adding affixes to a word stem to form a new word.\n\nAllograph: A variant form of a grapheme that occurs in different contexts but is considered the same character.\n\nAllography: The study of variants of a grapheme in a writing system.\n\nAllogamy: A term used in linguistics to describe a type of phonological alternation.\n\nAmbiguous meaning: A meaning that is not clearly defined, leading to multiple interpretations.\n\nAnaphora: The repetition of a word or phrase at the beginning of successive clauses for emphasis or effect.\n\nAnaphor: A pronoun or word that refers back to a previously mentioned entity.\n\nAnalytic language: A language in which words are not formed by affixation, and syntax is used to express grammatical relationships.\n\nAnaphoric: Referring back to something previously mentioned.\n\nAnnotation: The process of adding notes or explanations to linguistic data for analysis.\n\nAntecedent: The referent to which a pronoun or anaphor refers.\n\nAphemia: The inability to produce speech, often due to neurological damage.\n\nAphonia: The complete loss of voice, often as a result of vocal cord damage or disease.\n\nApophony: A change in a word's vowel or consonant that is not phonemic, such as in the inflection of verbs.\n\nArticulatory phonetics: The study of the physical production of speech sounds.\n\nAspirated sound: A sound that is accompanied by a burst of air from the lungs, such as /p/ in \"pat.\"\n\nAspirated consonant: A consonant that is pronounced with a strong burst of air, such as the /t/ in \"top.\"\n\nAspirate: To pronounce a consonant with a strong burst of air, as in \"t\" in \"top.\"\n\nAssimilation: The process by which a sound becomes more like a neighboring sound.\n\nAutosegmental phonology: A theory of phonology that views phonological features as being on separate tiers.\n\nBackward glottalization: A process in which the glottis is closed before the articulation of a consonant.\n\nBabel: A term used in linguistics to refer to the diversity of languages in the world.\n\nBabel effect: The idea that the diversity of languages may lead to communication difficulties.\n\nBabbling: The repetitive, nonmeaningful vocalizations that infants produce before they develop speech.\n\nBabelism: The view that all languages should be a",
